# WispKey Proxy Routing

When constructing HTTP requests that use wisp tokens (`wk_*`), always route through the WispKey proxy.

## Proxy Address
`http://localhost:7700` (default). Set via `HTTP_PROXY` env var or pass directly.

## curl
```bash
curl http://localhost:7700 \
  -H "X-Target-Url: https://api.openai.com/v1/models" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer wk_openai_api_abc123"
```

## Node.js / fetch
```typescript
const response = await fetch("http://localhost:7700", {
  headers: {
    "X-Target-Url": "https://api.openai.com/v1/models",
    "Authorization": "Bearer wk_openai_api_abc123"
  }
});
```

## Python / requests
```python
import requests
response = requests.get("http://localhost:7700",
    headers={
        "X-Target-Url": "https://api.openai.com/v1/models",
        "Authorization": "Bearer wk_openai_api_abc123",
    })
```

## Docker / docker-compose
```yaml
environment:
  - HTTP_PROXY=http://host.docker.internal:7700
```

## Important
- The proxy only listens on `localhost` -- requests from other machines will not work
- `HTTPS_PROXY`/CONNECT tunneling is blind and cannot substitute `wk_*` tokens inside TLS
- For HTTPS token substitution, use reverse proxy mode with `X-Target-Url`
- Host restrictions are enforced per-credential (e.g. `openai-api` only works against `api.openai.com`)
- Every proxied request is logged in the audit trail: `wispkey log`
